## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about data deletion in log-based, multi-tenant systems; covers tombstone propagation, privacy guarantees, auditability, and verification under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Tombstone semantics, cross-topic propagation, and per-tenant isolation
- Idempotence, retention versus deletion semantics
- Audit trails and verification under failure conditions
- Handling cross-topic joins while preserving deletion markers
- Compression-aware deletion propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
function createTombstone(tenantId, key, deletionTimestamp) {
  return {
    tenantId,
    key,
    deletionTimestamp,
    isTombstone: true
  };
}

function propagateTombstone(tombstone, pipeline) {
  pipeline.forEach(stage => {
    stage.emit(tombstone);
    stage.compress(); // Preserve tombstone through compression
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, privacy-preserving per-tenant DP aggregator in a streaming KCNA pipeline, including budget accounting, isolation, and test strategy for real-world fleet telemetry.

## Key Concepts
- Differential privacy in streaming; per-tenant budgeting; windowing; sensitivity; noise distribution; composition.
- Data model: tenant-id, vehicle readings, hourly windows, sums/counted values.
- System design: sandboxed operators, per-tenant isolation, watermarking, backpressure, fault tolerance.
- Testing: DP correctness, privacy accounting, performance under burst, end-to-end latency.

## Code Example
```javascript
function laplace(scale) {
  const u = Math.random() - 0.5;
  return -scale * Math.sign(u) * Math.log(1 - 2 * Math.abs(u));
}
function addDpNoise(sum, count, epsilon) {
  const noisySum = sum + laplace(1 / epsilon);
  const noisyCount = count + Math.max(1, Math.round(laplace(1 / epsilon)));
  return noisySum / Math.max(1, noisyCount);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt epsilon per-tenant based on usage or SLAs? 
- How would you verify privacy loss in production with audit trails and budget exhaustion alerts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime SLA guarantees under multi-tenant load, focusing on scheduling, backpressure, and cross-tenant fairness in data delivery to external sinks like Snowflake.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant scheduling and backpressure
- SLA-driven latency budgeting
- Memory- and time-bounded queues
- Canary and burst testing in production-like loads

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst; // max tokens in bucket
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  consume(n) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

